// M18 Phase 1: what a creature becomes when it dies -- by fire (fire.rs's
// ordinary `burns_into`, same mechanism every other flammable material
// uses) or by starvation (creature.rs's `die`). `kind: Powder`, not a
// special protected kind: a corpse is ordinary loose matter from here on,
// the same "destructible" the plan's own M18 verify criterion asks for --
// it falls under gravity, can be dug through, buried, or burned like any
// other powder, with no creature-specific handling left anywhere in the
// engine once this material takes over.
(
    name: "corpse",
    display: "Corpse",
    kind: Powder,
    density: 1.2,
    friction_angle: 30.0,
    // **Ordered darkest to lightest, and the order is load-bearing.**
    // `creature_dies` picks the shade by ramping a corpse's worth over this
    // list, so index order *is* the meaning: dark is a starved animal,
    // light
    // is one killed with energy still in the bank. Written the other way
    // round at first, which made a corpse worth 760 render darker than a
    // starved one worth 120 -- non-monotone, and the doc comment in
    // `creature.rs` confidently described the opposite of what shipped.
    //
    // **Five entries spanning 52..152 in red, not three spanning 84..104.**
    // The narrow version was correct and unreadable: a blind A/B at 10x
    // zoom
    // put six corpses worth 120..1020 side by side and they were one
    // colour.
    // The owner's verdict on the widened ramp -- "I can tell there is a
    // slightly different color, but that is pretty minor. Not much you can
    // do
    // if it is only two pixels though" -- is the reason this is as far as
    // colour goes here. It is taken because more legible beats less at no
    // cost, not because it makes the mechanic readable; a corpse is one or
    // two cells and `OrganismOverlay::FoodValue` is the readout that can
    // actually answer "how much".
    //
    // Order is load-bearing: `creature_dies` ramps worth over this list, so
    // dark is a starved animal and light is one killed with a full bank. It
    // shipped mid/dark/light once, which made a corpse worth 760 render
    // *darker* than a starved one worth 120.
    colors: [
        (52, 39, 33),
        (74, 56, 48),
        (96, 73, 63),
        (124, 95, 81),
        (152, 117, 100),
    ],
    flammability: 0.15,
    burn_temperature: 450.0,
    burn_duration: 90,
    burns_into: "ash",
    heat_conductivity: 0.1,

    // **Carrion rots into the ground it lies on**, and until 2026-09-06 it
    // was the one dead-organic material in the tree that did not.
    // `deadleaf`, `deadwood`, `log`, `windfall`, `litter` and `ash` all have
    // a `decays_into`; a corpse had none, so every animal that died and was
    // not eaten stood where it fell for the rest of the run.
    //
    // **The silence here used to be deliberate, and the condition it
    // depended on is now met.** `Reports/dead-ends.md` records it as
    // *"Decay needed no hook: `corpse.ron` declares no `decays_into`"* --
    // that is a statement about the **energy ledger**, not about carrion:
    // `EnergyLedger::meat_lost` books the three paths that destroy a stamped
    // cell so `max_standing_meat` is a bound rather than an estimate, and
    // decay was not one of them because decay could not reach a corpse.
    // `decay::tick` books it now, through the same shared
    // `EnergyLedger::meat_worth_of` predicate the other three call sites
    // use, so the bound survives the change.
    //
    // **What it costs to leave it out is both halves of the owner's report,
    // 2026-09-06.** A corpse is a `Powder`, so it falls -- into the gallery
    // an ant died in -- and a `Powder` that never decays is a plug that
    // never clears: measured on `examples/labnest` with an unfed colony,
    // **37 of 178 refilled void cells are corpse**, standing unchanged from
    // frame 6,000 to frame 18,000. And in a sealed box it is matter locked
    // out of the cycle: `examples/labmass control=empty colonies=1` books
    // **104 of 104 pool cells as `locked, no decays_into`, 100%**, so a bed
    // that has run a colony has permanently less ground in it.
    //
    // **`soil`, not `litter`**, and the chain argument `deadwood.ron` makes
    // does not carry here: a carcass is not leaf mould on its way to becoming
    // soil, and the middle this mechanic needs already exists on the other
    // axis -- a corpse is food, so it is eaten *or* it rots, which is the
    // graded outcome rather than one more state in a queue.
    decays_into: "soil",
    // **Litter's 0.05, for the reason `litter.ron` argues at length** and
    // `windfall.ron` already re-uses: rotting is microbial respiration and
    // the great majority of the mass leaves as gas this engine cannot
    // represent. An ant's body was bought with energy that came from a
    // plant, which fixed it out of light, so carrion is downstream of the
    // same source with no sink and wants the same humification efficiency.
    decay_yield: 0.05,
    // Faster than leaf litter (0.5/0.1) and a shade under fallen fruit
    // (0.7/0.15). Carrion is wet protein: in warm damp ground it is gone in
    // a fraction of the time a leaf takes, and in dry ground it desiccates
    // and keeps. At `decay::DECAY_TICK_INTERVAL` of 200 frames the damp rate
    // is a mean lifetime near 330 frames, which is short next to the 3,403
    // ticks `roster::how_long_a_cull_takes` measures for a body to reach the
    // graveyard -- a corpse is still there to be found and eaten, it just
    // does not stand for ever.
    decay_chance_damp: 0.6,
    decay_chance_dry: 0.12,
    // **No `reseed_chance`, so it defaults to 0** -- `litter.ron`'s argument,
    // and more so: a corpse falls where an animal died, which in this game is
    // disproportionately inside a nest, and sprouting a plant there would
    // wall the colony in with its own dead.

    // **The fallback for a corpse that arrived without a stamp**, which the
    // first version of this comment claimed could not happen. It can:
    // `ant.ron` sets `burns_into: "corpse"` and `fire.rs`'s burnout is
    // generic, so it writes a corpse cell with `aux` 0. At 0 that made a
    // burnt ant worth nothing to eat, against `wiki/ants.md`'s promise that
    // "ants that die in a fire become the next colony's dinner" -- a
    // player-visible mechanic silently deleted by an accounting change.
    //
    // 120 is one ant body cell (`ant.ron`'s `body_energy`), the smallest
    // real stamp in the game, so a body of unknown provenance is priced as
    // the cheapest thing it could have been rather than as the richest. A
    // burnt *beetle* is therefore under-priced at 120 against its own 200,
    // which is deliberate: fire destroying some of a carcass's worth is the
    // reading that costs nothing to believe, and the alternative is
    // teaching
    // a generic burnout path about creature species.
    food_energy: 480.0,
    // In MPa like every other value of this field. Carrion is dead cuticle,
    // and softer than the live cuticle at 0.25 -- which is what keeps the
    // scavenger niche the cheap meal.
    penetration_resistance: 0.1,
    food_class: 1.0,
    // Every corpse cell carries its own worth, stamped by `creature_dies`.
    worth_in_aux: true,
)
